      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey can u give me a quick breakdown of the stages in a standard CI/CD pipeline? specifically looking for code commit, testing, staging, and prod deployment. also lmk what kind of gates should be at each step to keep things clean.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "A standard CI/CD pipeline moves code through a series of stages, each guarded by specific gates to ensure quality and stability. It starts with a **Code Commit**, where a developer pushes changes to the repository. This is the first checkpoint, often guarded by a **Code Review Gate** (requiring at least one peer approval) and a **Linting Gate** (checking for syntax errors or style violations). If these pass, the code moves to the **Testing** stage, where automated unit and integration tests run. The gate here is the **Test Pass Gate**; if any test fails, the pipeline halts immediately, preventing broken code from moving forward. Once tests succeed, the code enters the **Staging** environment, which mimics production closely. This stage typically has a **Security Scan Gate** (checking for vulnerabilities) and a **Performance Gate** (ensuring the app runs fast enough). Finally, the code reaches **Production Deployment**. This is the most critical step, often requiring a **Change Approval Gate** (manual sign-off from a release manager) and a **Rollback Readiness Gate** (verifying that a quick rollback plan is in place).\n\nYou can think of this entire process as a **governed",
      "source": "target",
